-- ---- CROSS-CUTTING --------------------------------------------------------
(null, null, null, timestamptz '2026-09-18 00:00:00+00', 'research-agent-group-c', 1, 'unresolved',
 'SCOPE GAP: license_type ''DO'' is NOT COVERED in any of the twelve Group C states. In OH, IN, WI, MN, IA, NE, ND, SD and MT a single authority licenses MDs and DOs -- the State Medical Board of Ohio names "Physicians (MD, DO, DPM)" on the very page the Ohio fees were read from, Indiana''s PLA FAQ covers "Medical Doctor (MD) and Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO)" together, the Wisconsin DSPS fee schedule prints identical 10/31-odd-year $120 lines for MD and DO, and the South Dakota board is the Board of Medical AND OSTEOPATHIC Examiners. The MD rows are therefore LIKELY to hold for DOs in those states, but "likely" is not verified and no DO-specific page was read. ILLINOIS, KANSAS AND MISSOURI ARE DIFFERENT AND MORE DANGEROUS: Missouri''s osteopathic licensure sits in its own part of 20 CSR 2150 and Kansas and Illinois regulate osteopathy under separate provisions of the healing-arts and medical practice acts. A consumer that resolves a DO to the MD rows is producing an unverified obligation.'),

(null, null, null, timestamptz '2026-09-18 00:00:00+00', 'research-agent-group-c', 1, 'unresolved',
 'SCHEMA GAP, GROUP C RESTATEMENT: the field_key set still has no slot for the PERIODICITY of a mandated CE topic, and Group C makes it worse than the first four states did. Iowa runs TWO five-year topic clocks (CDC opioid guideline; end-of-life care) on top of a two-year licence. Minnesota runs a THREE-year CME clock on top of an ANNUAL licence -- the widest divergence in the table, and the one most likely to be silently mis-scheduled. Wisconsin scopes its opioid hours to a single named renewal. Nebraska publishes an opioid hour count with NO interval at all. Every one of these is carried as value_json with an explicit periodicity, and Nebraska''s additionally carries "periodicity_confirmed": false. ANY CONSUMER THAT READS value_num AND IGNORES value_json WILL SILENTLY DROP THESE REQUIREMENTS, and any consumer that anchors a topic clock to renewal_cycle_months will produce wrong dates for IA, MN and NY alike. Resolving this means a schema change, not a data change.'),

(null, null, null, timestamptz '2026-09-18 00:00:00+00', 'research-agent-group-c', 1, 'unresolved',
 'SCHEMA GAP, NP PRACTICE AUTHORITY: four of the twelve Group C states grant independent NP practice only after a transition-to-practice threshold, and they disagree on BOTH the number and the instrument -- Minnesota 2,080 hours under a COLLABORATIVE agreement (CNP and CNS only; CNMs and CRNAs exempt), Nebraska 2,000 hours under a written TRANSITION TO PRACTICE agreement with a SUPERVISING provider, South Dakota 1,040 hours under a COLLABORATIVE agreement retired on Form 3, and Wisconsin an unread threshold under its new APRN licensure. Missouri and Indiana require a collaborative/written practice agreement with NO threshold at all, and Ohio requires a standard care arrangement with no threshold. All four thresholds are carried in value_json and the instrument distinction is carried by using supervision_required for Nebraska and collaborative_agreement_required for the others; NEITHER IS COLLAPSED INTO THE OTHER ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE. What the schema still cannot express is that the SAME clinician crosses the threshold mid-cycle: the platform holds no per-clinician cumulative-practice-hours attribute, so no consumer can currently decide which side of a threshold a given NP is on. Until it does, every one of these rows is un-actionable per-clinician and should raise RULE_UNCERTAIN rather than generate an obligation.'),

(null, null, null, timestamptz '2026-09-18 00:00:00+00', 'research-agent-group-c', 1, 'unresolved',
 'SCHEMA/VOCABULARY GAP, GROUP C: five verified, recurring, board-published CE duties in these twelve states have NO home in the permitted topic vocabulary and are therefore absent from the requirements table entirely -- Ohio''s 1-hour duty-to-report-misconduct CME (seeded under ce_topic_ethics_hours as the nearest fit, and flagged), Missouri''s 1-hour health-benefits-of-nutrition CME, Iowa''s five-yearly end-of-life care training (seeded under ce_topic_pain_management_hours as the nearest fit, and flagged), Iowa''s five-yearly abuse-identification and mandatory-reporter training, and Illinois''s 1-hour Alzheimer''s disease and other dementias CME for both MDs and APRNs treating patients aged 26 and over (a conditional-population requirement published on IDFPR''s Continuing Education page and in the physician and APRN packets). The Illinois sexual-harassment-prevention hour, required of both professions every cycle, is likewise unmappable. THESE ARE REAL OBLIGATIONS A CLIENT WILL BE AUDITED ON. Adding ce_topic_nutrition_hours, ce_topic_dementia_hours, ce_topic_sexual_harassment_hours, ce_topic_end_of_life_hours and ce_topic_mandatory_reporter_hours would close it; inventing them unilaterally in a data seed would not.'),

(null, null, null, timestamptz '2026-09-18 00:00:00+00', 'research-agent-group-c', 1, 'unresolved',
 'ACCESS GAP, GROUP C SUMMARY -- the sources that could NOT be read on 2026-09-18, and which therefore account for most of the empty cells above. ROBOTS/TLS REFUSAL: ilga.gov in every URL form tried (Illinois Medical Practice Act, Nurse Practice Act, and all of 68 Ill. Adm. Code 1285 and 1300); docs.legis.wisconsin.gov (Wis. Admin. Code ch. Med 13, and 2023 AB 154 / SB 145); ksrevisor.gov K.S.A. 65-1130; rules.mt.gov (HTTP 405 on ARM 24.156.2718). BOT BLOCKING: revisor.mo.gov after two requests ("appears a bot-like access pattern was detected"), which is why the Missouri physician renewal cycle is unseeded. JAVASCRIPT-ONLY PORTALS serving no static text: iar.iga.in.gov (848 IAC 5, Indiana APRN prescriptive authority); nursing.ohio.gov (Board of Nursing fee schedule and APRN practice pages); idfpr.illinois.gov/dpr/license-renewal-info.html (the expiry-date table); sdlegislature.gov''s HTML rule and statute viewers, though its /api/Rules/Rule/<article>.html?all=true endpoint DID work and is how the South Dakota rows were obtained. DEAD LINKS: idfpr.illinois.gov .../aprn-fpa.pdf, in.gov/pla .../continuing-education-requirements/ and .../advanced-practice-nurse/, mn.gov/boards/medical-practice/licensing/fees/, boards.bsd.dli.mt.gov .../aprn/prescriptive-authority -- all 404. Every one of these is retrievable by hand or by a request to the board; none of them was guessed around.');

commit;
